StatGraphs -- a package for graphs used in statistical models
*************************************************************

Description
===========

This package contains the types of graphs that are used in algebraic
statistics: *note Bigraph: Bigraph, and  *note MixedGraph: MixedGraph,.

A bigraph is graph with bidirected edges and no multiple edges. A mixed graph
is a graph with undirected, directed and bidirected edges.

This is an example of a bigraph on 4 vertices. It is created using the method
*note bigraph: bigraph,.



+------------------------------------+
|i1 : G = bigraph {{3,4},{1,2},{2,4}}|
|                                    |
|o1 = Bigraph{1 => {2}   }           |
|             2 => {4, 1}            |
|             3 => {4}               |
|             4 => {3, 2}            |
|                                    |
|o1 : Bigraph                        |
+------------------------------------+

Next is an example of a mixed graph on 4 vertices with undirected, directed and
bidirected edges. It is created using the method *note mixedGraph: mixedGraph,.



+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : G = mixedGraph(graph{{1,2}},digraph {{1,3},{2,3}},bigraph {{3,4}})|
|                                                                       |
|o2 = MixedGraph{Bigraph => Bigraph{3 => {4}}}                          |
|                                   4 => {3}                            |
|                Digraph => Digraph{1 => {3}}                           |
|                                   2 => {3}                            |
|                                   3 => {}                             |
|                Graph => Graph{1 => {2}}                               |
|                               2 => {1}                                |
|                                                                       |
|o2 : MixedGraph                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------+

One can extract key information about mixed graphs using: *note
undirectedGraph(MixedGraph): undirectedGraph,, *note bigraph(MixedGraph):
bigraph_lpMixedGraph_rp,, *note digraph(MixedGraph): digraph_lpMixedGraph_rp,,
*note vertices(MixedGraph): vertices_lpMixedGraph_rp,,

or convert a mixed graph into a more convenient form using: *note
collateVertices(MixedGraph): collateVertices,, *note
indexLabelGraph(MixedGraph): indexLabelGraph_lpMixedGraph_rp,.

There are several methods that allow to check the properties of mixed graphs:
*note isCyclic(MixedGraph): isCyclic_lpMixedGraph_rp,, *note
isLoopless(MixedGraph): isLoopless,, *note isSimple(MixedGraph):
isSimple_lpMixedGraph_rp,.

One can also study the properties of vertices using: *note
children(MixedGraph,Thing): children_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,, *note
parents(MixedGraph,Thing): parents_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,, *note
descendants(MixedGraph,Thing): descendants_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,, *note
nondescendants(MixedGraph,Thing): nondescendants_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,,
*note forefathers(MixedGraph,Thing): forefathers_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,,
*note neighbors(MixedGraph,Thing): neighbors_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,, *note
nonneighbors(MixedGraph,Thing): nonneighbors_lpMixedGraph_cmThing_rp,.

This package allows to construct loopless mixed graphs (LMG) as introduced in
Kayvan Sadeghi and Steffen Lauritzen, Markov properties for mixed graphs,
Bernoulli 20.2 (2014): 676-696 and to check whether a mixedGraph is an LMG
using *note partitionLMG: partitionLMG,.



Caveat
======

StatGraphs requires  *note Graphs: (Graphs)Top, version 0.3.3 or later.



